rrent step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} | {{field-5}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n  {{field-6}}." 13194139535013 true] [17592186326755 1113 #uuid "bf4e7763-90ca-5b57-9b3f-85f8d450ddda" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326755 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the $2$-step ABM method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}-2y \\\\  & =3({2}^{2})-2(\\frac{15}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=3.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{21}{4}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =3({3}^{2})-2(9) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 9+8\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}-3)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 78 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=3.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{13}{2}=\\frac{41}{4}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $\\frac{21}{4}$ | $9$ | $9$ | $\\frac{13}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $\\frac{41}{4}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(3)≈\\frac{41}{4}.$" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326757 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326758 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326759 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1041 "field-1" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326758 13194139535013 true] [17592186326756 1110 #uuid "5d0954dc-cf4c-5513-8cd9-a10f0cc29fc7" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326757 1034 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326757 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535013 true] [17592186326757 1111 #uuid "18f29e72-a240-5743-a15c-fbf6d9937243" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326758 1034 "\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326758 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535013 true] [17592186326758 1111 #uuid "86044bfc-32eb-59dd-a199-fb27d8c225e1" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326759 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326759 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535013 true] [17592186326759 1111 #uuid "192a1c8f-d922-5134-8c3e-4f224e1f4543" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326760 1000 #edb/ref 1759218632