oulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326780 1113 #uuid "86981e18-3dad-5ccc-bfe2-8e7ddf358746" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326780 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =6{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,\\frac{7}{3})$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x-2y \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{7}{3}+3=\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =6{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+8\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{58}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{29}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{29}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{71}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{16}{3}$ | $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $\\frac{29}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{71}{18}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{71}{18}.$" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326782 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326783 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326784 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326785 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326786 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1041 "selection" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326785 13194139535013 true] [17592186326781 1110 #uuid "527d6df4-b5f0-5f2e-8a38-9932b5908545" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326782 1034 "\\frac{73}{18}" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326782 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535013 true] [17592186326782 1111 #uuid "1b857d5b-fa88-55c0-9739-710156523f59" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326783 1034 "\\frac{75}{18}" 13194139535013 true] [17592186326783 1103 #edb/ref 175921860454