 & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =3{x}_{-1}-4{y}_{-1}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-4\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-4y-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 2-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-8)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}-4{y}_{p}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-6)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{59}{2}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding th